{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Test` - the runner - Design-Test.md §4.1, §5 and §6.
   ==========================================================================================

   The four types a `Case` is made of, and the function the synthesized entry hands its cases to.
   `Case`'s five fields are the contract between the compiler and this module - `resolveTestEntry`
   fills them by position and says so where it reads the shape - so the record's *shape* may not
   move without the compiler moving with it. Everything else here is the library's.

   The shapes are §4.1's, with one rename: the field is `moduleName`, because `module` is a reserved
   word and `data Case {module: String}` does not parse.

   `Site`'s strings are constants. A string literal is a borrowed run over a constant global and its
   teardown is nothing at all, so a `Site` costs some constant data and no runtime work - which is
   what makes it free to put one on every case and, through `@caller`, on every assertion.

   The vocabulary a test is *written* in is in the files beside this one: `Check.yana` is §4.2 and
   §4.3, `Section.yana` is §4.5, `Report.yana` is §5.1 and §5.2, and `Harness.yana` is §5.3's third
   tier and §6.
-}

import Native

{-
   Where something is, filled by the compiler.

   The field *names* are the contract rather than the type: `@caller` matches a location record by
   name against a fixed vocabulary - `file` and `function` are text, `line` and `column` are numbers -
   because the compiler knows nothing about this declaration in particular. A test framework that
   wanted only the line would declare a record with only `line` in it and get one.
-}
pub data Site {file: String, line: Int, column: Int, function: String}

-- One claim that did not hold. `detail` is the second line a report gets - a hexdump, a diff - and is
-- empty for the assertions that have nothing to add.
pub data Failure {site: Site, claim: String, detail: String}

{-
   What a case answers.

   `Result` and not a type of its own, so that `?` works on it out of the box: `Core`'s `Try` class
   already gives `requireEqual(...)?` its meaning, and a test that returns early at the first thing
   that did not hold is that operator and nothing else.
-}
pub alias Test = Result(Failure, {})

{-
   One case, as the compiler hands it over.

   Everything here is known at the declaration and nothing is discovered at run time, which is the
   whole argument for the entry being synthesized: a registration list would carry the same five
   fields and would be missing for whichever test somebody forgot to add to it.
-}
pub data Case {name: String, moduleName: String, site: Site, aborts: Bool, body: () -> Test}

-- What a report line and a `--only` name a case by. The module and the declaration, which is unique
-- by construction: two `@test`s of one name in one module do not compile.
pub fn qualifiedName(self: Case) -> String = "{self.moduleName}.{self.name}"

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The runner
   ==========================================================================================

   `runMain` is what the synthesized entry calls and what the program's status is. Three things
   happen here and each is a section of the design:

    1. the command line decides *which* cases run and *where* - §5.3's three isolation tiers and
       §6's sharding, which are one mechanism: `--isolate` is `--shard i/n` with `n` the number of
       cases, and both are this binary re-executing itself;
    2. each case that runs in this process is bracketed by a `begin` and an `end` on the report
       stream, which is what makes a crash attributable at no cost - §5.2;
    3. the count is printed and the status is 0 or 1.

   **A run that discovered no cases is a failure**, which is `test/README.md`'s empty-corpus guard
   said in the new framework's terms: a driver that verified nothing must not be mistaken for one
   that verified everything, and a test binary that ran zero cases is exactly that mistake. So is a
   `--filter` that matched nothing, unless `--allow-empty` says otherwise.
-}
pub fn runMain(cases: [Case]) -> Int:
    let options = readOptions(runnerArguments())

    if length(options.complaint) > (0 :: Size):
        print("{options.complaint}\n")
        return 2

    if length(cases) == (0 :: Size):
        print("no tests: a build that discovered no cases is a failure, not a pass\n")
        return 1

    -- Read once, before any case runs, so that a snapshot written from a helper five frames down
    -- still lands where the run was told to put it - see Golden.yana.
    configureSnapshots(options)

    -- Refused rather than quietly ignored. `--isolate` is what a suite with global state, or a suite
    -- that crashes, is run with, and a green run under a flag that did nothing is worse than a red
    -- one. An `@test(aborts)` case is the softer case and is skipped - see below.
    if (options.isolate || options.jobs > 1) && !harnessAvailable():
        print("this target has no harness, so --isolate and --jobs have nothing to spawn\n")
        return 2

    let &selected = [] :: [Size]
    let &ordinal = 0 :: Int

    for i in 0 .. length(cases):
        if selects(options, cases[i].qualifiedName()):
            -- The shard is applied to the *filtered* ordinal, so that four shards of a filtered run
            -- still divide that run's work between them rather than three of them idling.
            if options.shards <= 1 || ordinal % options.shards == options.shard then push(selected, i)
            ordinal = ordinal + 1

    if options.list:
        for at in items(selected):
            print("{cases[at].qualifiedName()}\n")

        return 0

    if length(selected) == (0 :: Size) && !options.allowEmpty:
        print("no tests matched, which is a failure rather than a pass - pass --allow-empty if it is not\n")
        return 1

    return runSelected(cases, selected, options)

{-
   The selected cases, run wherever they belong.

   Three destinations, and which one a case reaches is decided per case rather than per run:

    - a `@test(aborts)` case always goes to a child, whatever the run's default is, because its
      verdict *is* the child's status and there is nothing to catch in-process;
    - every case goes to a child under `--isolate`;
    - everything else runs here, in order, in this process.

   A run that asked for children on a target with no harness says so and fails, rather than running
   the cases in-process and reporting a pass: `--isolate` is what a suite with global state or a
   suite that crashes is run with, and quietly declining it would answer a question nobody asked.
-}
fn runSelected(cases: [Case], selected: [Size], options: Options) -> Int:
    if options.jobs > 1 && length(options.only) == (0 :: Size) then return runShards(cases, options)

    {-
       **A process whose report has a channel of its own prints nothing else.**

       Descriptor 3 being open means something started this process in order to read its report, and
       that something is what reports for a human - so saying it here as well is how the first
       isolated run printed every failure once per process, and how a driver that JITs a fixture and
       forks got a summary line per build. A binary run at a terminal has no 3, and prints.

       `--only` is the same fact from the other side: it is what the harness re-executes with, and a
       run narrowed to one case is a child whatever its descriptors say.
    -}
    let silent = length(options.only) > (0 :: Size) || reportIsPrivate()

    {-
       Whether this process is the one a child was asked for.

       Separate from `silent`, and the two were one for a while: a *shard* is silent - the parent
       reads its stream - and a shard still has to be able to isolate its own cases. What may never
       spawn again is a run narrowed to a single case, because that is what `runChild` re-executes
       with and an `@test(aborts)` case would otherwise ask for a child whose first act is to ask
       for a child. That is not a hypothetical: it is a fork bomb, and it is what the first version
       of this line was.
    -}
    let isOneCase = length(options.only) > (0 :: Size)

    {-
       Where this run reports to a person - §5.5 and `Console.yana`.

       Built whether or not it will print, and it is `silent` that decides which: a console that is
       not reporting answers every call at its first line, so the loop below has no second shape and
       the rule about who prints stays the one sentence above rather than being spread over the ten
       places that used to test it.
    -}
    let title = sharedModule(cases, selected)
    let &console = openConsole(options, title, length(selected), !silent)

    -- The console keeps the counts a report is made of - it is the thing that prints them. What is
    -- still counted here is the one number the *status* is made of, which is nobody else's.
    let &failedCases = 0 :: Int

    for j in 0 .. length(selected):
        let case = cases[selected[j]]
        let name = case.qualifiedName()
        let index = truncate(selected[j]) :: Int

        console.caseBegan(name)

        if case.aborts && !harnessAvailable():
            {-
               An expected abort with nowhere to run - the JavaScript target, per §11.2's F4 note.

               Skipped and counted as skipped, rather than failed. The case is fine; this target
               cannot ask the question, and a suite that went red on every `@test(aborts)` because
               of where it was run would be reporting the runner rather than the library.
            -}
            let why = "@test(aborts) needs a harness and this target has none"
            reportLine("skip {index} {name} - {why}\n")
            console.caseSkipped(name, why)
        else if (case.aborts || options.isolate) && !isOneCase:
            {-
               A case run in a child is still bracketed *here*.

               The child writes its own stream into its own file and the parent reads it, so the
               `begin` and `end` a reader of *this* process's stream sees would otherwise be missing
               for exactly the cases that were isolated - and a shard whose report is short by its
               isolated cases is a shard whose count does not add up. The verdict is the child's; the
               two lines around it are this process's.
            -}
            beginCase(index, name)
            let started = monotonicNanos()
            let verdict = runChild(name, case.aborts, options)
            let elapsed = monotonicNanos() - started

            match verdict:
                Ok(->note) -> console.casePassed(name, elapsed, note)

                Err(->why):
                    recordAt(case.site, why, "")
                    failedCases = failedCases + 1
                    console.caseFailed(name, elapsed, why)

            endCase(if currentFailures() == 0 then "pass" else "fail", elapsed)
        else:
            beginCase(index, name)
            let started = monotonicNanos()

            {-
               An `Err` the body handed back, reported only when nothing else was.

               A `require…` records its failure *and* answers `Err`, so the stream already has the
               line - with its section labels, at the moment it happened - and saying it again here
               would double every early return. What this catches is the other `Err`: a `?` on a
               `File` call that failed, which nothing recorded because nothing in this library was
               involved.
            -}
            match case.body():
                Ok(_) -> {}
                Err(failure) -> if currentFailures() == 0 then record(failure)

            let elapsed = monotonicNanos() - started
            let lost = currentFailures()
            endCase(if lost == 0 then "pass" else "fail", elapsed)

            if lost == 0:
                console.casePassed(name, elapsed, "")
            else:
                failedCases = failedCases + 1
                console.caseFailed(name, elapsed, "{lost} of its claims did not hold")

    {-
       §7's ruling 3, asked only of a run that reached everything.

       A filtered run, a shard, and a run whose cases went to children have each reached some of the
       snapshots by construction, so calling the rest stale would be reporting the *selection*. A
       stale one fails the run, on the empty-corpus guard's argument: a golden nothing compares
       against is indistinguishable from one that passes.
    -}
    let whole = length(selected) == length(cases) && !options.isolate && options.jobs <= 1
    let stale = reviewSnapshots(whole)

    {-
       The run, as one screen - the table, the failures, the counts and the time.

       This is also where the status line is taken back off the screen, whatever else it prints, so
       a run that is not reporting still leaves the cursor at the margin.
    -}
    console.close(stale, snapshotSummary())

    if failedCases == 0 && stale == 0 then return 0
    return 1
